From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 16:52:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: name the section a stalled boot stopped
 in

A J604 boot can stop between about 6 and 15 seconds of uptime with
nothing logged: no CPU schedules the watchdog keepalive any more, so the
watchdog resets the SoC 30 seconds after Linux took it over. The journal
holds only what was synced, and the ramoops region does not survive the
reset. The PMU scratch does, and macsmc-reboot already reads it.

Add boot crumbs. For the first 20 seconds of a boot, each instrumented
section marks itself entered in the PMU scratch before it runs and left
after it ran, and a 1 Hz heartbeat records the last uptime second. The
write happens at the call when the caller may sleep and from a work item
otherwise. After 20 seconds nothing is written. macsmc-reboot reads the
previous boot's record before this boot writes, prints which sections
were entered and not left and when the heartbeat stopped, and clears the
record on a clean shutdown, so only a boot that did not shut down
cleanly is reported.

The sections are what runs between 4.5 and 16 seconds in a good J604
boot: the external DCPs' power-down and power-up, connector detect on an
external DCP, appledrm open and atomic commit, GPU start (its first
power-up included), brcmfmac preinit and country update, SEP attach and
the SEP sensor probe, the ANE idle power-down, the tas2764 fault
handler, the PMP thermal sample, macaudio and AVD open, and the
pcie-apple port interrupt. The record is 6 bytes at 0xf840 in the
primary PMU's legacy scratch, where a live read showed only zeros.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: LLM
